### What is peek and what does it print?

`peek` takes a `Consumer<T>` and runs it on each element as that element passes through the peek stage, then returns the same element to the next stage. Its signature is `Stream<T> peek(Consumer<? super T> action)`. The consumer usually logs or prints, so you can see each element the pipeline processes.

```java
package com.bank.debug;

import java.math.BigDecimal;
import java.util.List;
import java.util.stream.Collectors;

public class PeekPrint {
    record Employee(String name, BigDecimal salary) {}

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        List<Employee> staff = List.of(
            new Employee("Rajesh", new BigDecimal("60000")),
            new Employee("Priya", new BigDecimal("45000"))
        );

        List<Employee> result = staff.stream()
            .peek(e -> System.out.println("processing: " + e.name()))
            .collect(Collectors.toList());

        System.out.println("collected " + result.size() + " employees");
    }
}
```

```text
processing: Rajesh
processing: Priya
collected 2 employees
```

### Why use peek for debugging instead of breaking the pipeline?

Without peek you would have to split the pipeline, collect to an intermediate list, print it, and rebuild the rest. peek lets you insert a single observation line between any two stages and remove it later, keeping the pipeline fluent while you investigate.

```java
// Before: break the pipeline to inspect, then rebuild
List<Employee> survivors = staff.stream()
    .filter(e -> e.salary().compareTo(new BigDecimal("50000")) > 0)
    .collect(Collectors.toList());
System.out.println(survivors); // manual inspection step
List<String> names = survivors.stream().map(Employee::name).collect(Collectors.toList());

// After: inspect in place with peek, in one pipeline
List<String> namesWithPeek = staff.stream()
    .filter(e -> e.salary().compareTo(new BigDecimal("50000")) > 0)
    .peek(e -> System.out.println("survivor: " + e.name()))
    .map(Employee::name)
    .collect(Collectors.toList());
```

### How does the lazy nature of peek affect when it runs?

peek is intermediate, so it is lazy. The consumer runs only when a terminal operation pulls elements. A pipeline that ends at peek with no terminal does nothing at all.

```java
package com.bank.debug;

import java.util.List;

public class PeekLazy {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        // No terminal: peek never runs
        List.of("Rajesh", "Priya").stream()
            .peek(n -> System.out.println("peeked: " + n));
        System.out.println("nothing was peeked above");

        // Add a terminal: now peek runs
        long count = List.of("Rajesh", "Priya").stream()
            .peek(n -> System.out.println("peeked: " + n))
            .filter(n -> n.length() > 4)
            .count();
        System.out.println("count = " + count);
    }
}
```

```text
nothing was peeked above
peeked: Rajesh
peeked: Priya
count = 2
```

### How do you place peek before and after a filter to diagnose a wrong count?

This is the core debugging pattern. A peek before the filter logs every incoming employee; a peek after the filter logs only the survivors. Comparing the two logs tells you exactly which records were dropped.

```java
package com.bank.debug;

import java.math.BigDecimal;
import java.util.List;
import java.util.stream.Collectors;

public class DiagnoseFilter {
    record Employee(String name, BigDecimal salary) {}

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        List<Employee> staff = List.of(
            new Employee("Rajesh", new BigDecimal("60000")),
            new Employee("Priya", new BigDecimal("45000")),
            new Employee("Amit", new BigDecimal("80000"))
        );

        BigDecimal threshold = new BigDecimal("50000");

        long highEarners = staff.stream()
            .peek(e -> System.out.println("IN : " + e.name() + " = " + e.salary()))
            .filter(e -> e.salary().compareTo(threshold) > 0)
            .peek(e -> System.out.println("OUT: " + e.name() + " survived"))
            .count();

        System.out.println("high earners = " + highEarners);
    }
}
```

```text
IN : Rajesh = 60000
OUT: Rajesh survived
IN : Priya = 45000
IN : Amit = 80000
OUT: Amit survived
high earners = 2
```

The log shows Priya came in but never survived, so you instantly see the filter dropped her at 45,000.

### How does interleaving work when you have peek at many stages?

Streams process one element all the way through before starting the next, not stage by stage. The log interleaving makes this visible and is often itself the thing a developer learns from peek.

```java
package com.bank.debug;

import java.util.List;
import java.util.stream.Collectors;

public class PeekInterleaving {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        String joined = List.of("acc-1", "acc-2").stream()
            .peek(a -> System.out.println("1 source: " + a))
            .map(String::toUpperCase)
            .peek(a -> System.out.println("2 mapped: " + a))
            .collect(Collectors.joining(","));
        System.out.println(joined);
    }
}
```

```text
1 source: acc-1
2 mapped: ACC-1
1 source: acc-2
2 mapped: ACC-2
ACC-1,ACC-2
```

### How is peek different from forEach and map?

peek observes and continues; forEach ends the pipeline; map transforms. Using the wrong one is a frequent source of confusion.

| Operation | Type | Ends pipeline? | Transforms? | Typical use |
|-----------|------|----------------|-------------|-------------|
| `peek(Consumer)` | intermediate | no | no | debug logging between stages |
| `map(Function)` | intermediate | no | yes | convert element to a new value |
| `forEach(Consumer)` | terminal | yes | no | run a side effect at the end |

```java
List<String> ids = List.of("a", "b");

// peek: keeps the stream going
ids.stream().peek(System.out::println).count();

// forEach: terminal, returns void, cannot chain after it
ids.stream().forEach(System.out::println);

// map: transforms, so downstream sees changed values
List<String> upper = ids.stream().map(String::toUpperCase).collect(java.util.stream.Collectors.toList());
```

### What are common mistakes when debugging with peek?

The common mistake is leaving peek in production or putting real work inside it. peek is for temporary observation; business effects do not belong there.

```java
// WRONG: using peek to do the work, then forgetting to remove it
staff.stream()
    .peek(e -> auditRepository.save(e)) // effect may be skipped, and ships to prod
    .count();

// WRONG: mutating the element inside a debug peek
staff.stream()
    .peek(e -> e.setProcessed(true))    // hidden mutation in a debug tool
    .collect(Collectors.toList());
```

```java
// CORRECT: peek only logs, and it is removed before shipping
staff.stream()
    .peek(e -> log.fine(() -> "debug: processing " + e.name())) // advisory only
    .collect(Collectors.toList());

// CORRECT: persistence runs in a guaranteed terminal stage
staff.forEach(auditRepository::save);
```

### What is the edge case where count skips peek?

Since Java 9, if the stream is sized and the terminal is `count()`, the runtime returns the size without traversing elements, so an upstream peek never runs. This is why you must not trust peek to run for every element.

```java
package com.bank.debug;

import java.util.List;

public class PeekSkippedByCount {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        long n = List.of("a", "b", "c").stream()
            .peek(x -> System.out.println("peeked: " + x)) // Java 9+: skipped
            .count();
        System.out.println("count = " + n);
    }
}
```

```text
count = 3
```

Nothing from peek printed, because the size was known without walking the elements.

### When should you use peek and when should you remove it?

Use peek while you are diagnosing a pipeline: to see each element, to count survivors at each stage, or to understand interleaving. Keep it short and non-essential. Once the investigation is done, remove peek so production code does not depend on a stage the contract may skip and so logs stay clean.

```java
// During investigation
report = staff.stream()
    .peek(e -> System.out.println("debug: " + e))   // temporary
    .filter(e -> e.salary().compareTo(threshold) > 0)
    .collect(Collectors.toList());

// Shipped code: peek removed
report = staff.stream()
    .filter(e -> e.salary().compareTo(threshold) > 0)
    .collect(Collectors.toList());
```

### What are common interview follow-up questions?

Expect: Will peek run if there is no terminal operation? Answer: no, because intermediate operations are lazy. Why might a peek before count print nothing? Answer: a sized stream with count can skip traversal since Java 9. How do you use peek to find why a filter drops records? Answer: place peek before and after the filter and compare the two logs. Should peek ever persist data or mutate elements? Answer: no, those are side effects that belong in map or forEach, because peek is a debugging-only observation stage you remove before production.
